So far so good to use C constructs (a raw syntax, but effective).

> To implement inheritance, define a new struct with the base struct as
> the first member.

Containment is not inheritance (even if using function pointers).

> Then, a cast (struct base *) will convert a derived
> pointer to a base pointer.

OO concepts and static type casting are completely different concepts;
one of the central OO characteristic is polymorphism.

> Virtual functions can be implemented with function pointers.

Not really. (See casting above.)

> There is nothing about the C language itself which prevents OO design.

Well, you can structure your code in some way to achieve information
hiding, can associate data and functions in file packages as modules.
That's good.

But to implement an OO design with real inheritance and polymorphism,
and all that one would have to implement all the low level OO
administration code (something like C++'s virtual function tables, for
example) explicitly.

The essence is that you can of course write kind of OO even in some
assembler, but the point of OO languages is to support the concept
without bothering the programmer with the technical implementation
internally necessary to support object orientation.

> It's a little clunky, but it works and is readable and maintainable. I
> don't understand what you have against this idea.

You can of course emulate some OO concepts by clunky constructs, and
organize them in a sophisticated and relative readable way. Not that
I'd call the outcome of an OO emulation in a low level language like C
as "maintainable", though, rather extreemly error prone.

